# Backstory

Silas Quickthorn wasn’t just raised by the thieves’ guild — he was engineered by them.

Found as a tiny goblin child curled inside a drainpipe, the guildmaster saw potential where others saw a nuisance. He took the child in, not out of kindness, but ambition. A goblin small enough to slip through cracks, clever enough to survive alone, and desperate enough to cling to any form of belonging.

From the moment Silas could walk, the guildmaster began shaping him. He taught him silence, discipline, and patience. Locks before letters. Shadows before sunlight. Loyalty before morality.

The guild didn’t give Silas toys — they gave him tools.

At five, he learned to pick locks with wires thinner than hair.  
At six, he could throw a dagger with enough precision to split a candlewick.  
At seven, he was slipping through windows grown thieves couldn’t fit through.

The guildmaster named him **Silas Quickthorn**, a name meant to cut, meant to sting, meant to remind the guild that this child was not a pet — but a weapon.

As he grew, Silas mastered every tool the guild relied on: twin daggers, throwing knives, smokebombs, flashbombs, alchemist’s fire, spyglasses, and wire coils. The guildmaster even gave him a slim black dagger — a personal gift, or a leash. Silas still isn’t sure.

By adolescence, Silas had become the guild’s secret infiltrator. The one they sent into noble estates, guarded vaults, and locked towers. The one who could slip through cracks, vanish into rafters, and leave no trace except the whisper:

**“Quickthorn was here.”**

His missions were so clean, so silent, so deadly, that guards began telling stories of a phantom thief — a creature who appeared only long enough for a glint of steel before vanishing again.

It started with one terrified guard who swore he saw “a little ghost” slip past him without a sound.

The name spread through taverns, barracks, caravans, and eventually across the lands:

**The Little Ghost.**

A goblin assassin who kills without being seen.  
A shadow that moves like smoke.  
A myth whispered by those who survived him.

Now grown, Silas carries every skill and tool he was taught. He moves with the discipline drilled into him since childhood. But he also carries questions — about who he is, who he was meant to be, and whether the guild raised him… or claimed him.

The guild still watches him.  
And they always collect their debts.
